<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

JavaScript can add small interactions, visualizations, or course-specific
controls to a CAT-SOOP page.  A page can contain a `script` element directly,
load a file from `__STATIC__`, or append script markup to `cs_scripts`.

Use JavaScript to enhance a page, not to enforce permissions or perform
trusted grading.  Everything sent to the browser can be viewed and modified by
the user; authorization and protected state changes must remain on the server.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>An Inline Script in Page Content</catsoop-section>

For a short interaction used by one page, place the markup and script directly
in `content.catsoop` or `content.md`:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;button type="button" id="nm-reveal"&gt;Show explanation&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;div id="nm-explanation" hidden&gt;
  The method converges because each iteration reduces the residual.
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
(() =&gt; {
  const button = document.getElementById("nm-reveal");
  const explanation = document.getElementById("nm-explanation");

  button.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
    explanation.hidden = !explanation.hidden;
    button.textContent = explanation.hidden
      ? "Show explanation"
      : "Hide explanation";
  });
})();
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

The script appears after the elements it uses, so those elements already exist
when the browser runs it.  Keep the opening and closing tags together and
always use a full closing `</script>` tag.  In HTML, `script` is not a
self-closing element.

Direct scripts remain at their position in the page body.  This makes them
convenient for a small, page-local enhancement.  Move longer code to a
separate file so it can be syntax-checked, cached by the browser, and reused.

<catsoop-section>Loading a JavaScript File</catsoop-section>

Put the file below the page's `__STATIC__` directory:

```text
homework/
└── hw01/
    ├── content.catsoop
    └── __STATIC__/
        └── js/
            └── convergence-controls.js
```

Then load it from the page:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;script src="CURRENT/js/convergence-controls.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

`CURRENT` is rewritten relative to the current page.  A shared file in the
course root can instead be stored at
`COURSE_ROOT/__STATIC__/js/course-tools.js` and loaded with:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;script src="COURSE/js/course-tools.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

See [Images and Other Static Content](COURSE/docs/authoring/static_content) for
the complete `__STATIC__`, `CURRENT`, and `COURSE` rules.

The `defer` attribute downloads an ordinary external script without blocking
HTML parsing and runs it after the document has been parsed.  It is a good
default for code that needs elements from the page body.

A corresponding file might contain:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
  const plot = document.getElementById("nm-convergence-plot");
  if (!plot) return;

  plot.addEventListener("click", () => {
    plot.classList.toggle("nm-expanded");
  });
});
```

Checking for the element makes the file safe to include on a group of pages
where only some pages contain that interaction.

<catsoop-section>Adding Scripts With `cs_scripts`</catsoop-section>

`cs_scripts` is a string of raw HTML that CAT-SOOP inserts into the document's
`head` element.  Append to it in `preload.py` when every page below that
preload should receive the same script:

<pre><code class="language-python">
cs_scripts += """
&lt;script src="COURSE/js/course-tools.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
"""
</code></pre>

Use `+=`, not `=`, so that scripts inherited from parent `preload.py` files are
preserved.  A course-root preload affects the whole course; a preload in
`homework/hw01` affects that page and its descendants.

In a page-specific preload, a `CURRENT` URL is also possible:

<pre><code class="language-python">
cs_scripts += """
&lt;script src="CURRENT/js/convergence-controls.js" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
"""
</code></pre>

Remember that `CURRENT` is resolved against the page being rendered, not
against the filesystem location of the `preload.py` file.  In an inherited
preload, prefer a stable `COURSE/...` URL unless every descendant has the same
relative static-file layout.

Small inline setup can also be added to the head:

<pre><code class="language-python">
cs_scripts += """
&lt;script&gt;
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () =&gt; {
  document.querySelectorAll("[data-nm-confirm]").forEach((button) =&gt; {
    button.addEventListener("click", (event) =&gt; {
      if (!window.confirm(button.dataset.nmConfirm)) {
        event.preventDefault();
      }
    });
  });
});
&lt;/script&gt;
"""
</code></pre>

Because `cs_scripts` is placed in the head, inline code that reads page-body
elements should wait for `DOMContentLoaded`.  Do not assign
`window.onload = ...`; use `addEventListener` so that CAT-SOOP and other course
scripts can keep their own load listeners.

The same `cs_scripts += ...` operation can be performed in a page Python block
or a Python-generated page.  `preload.py` is preferable when the behavior is
configuration shared by a page subtree.

<catsoop-section>ES Modules</catsoop-section>

Modern, self-contained code can use a module script:

<pre><code class="language-html">
&lt;script type="module" src="CURRENT/js/simulation.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Module variables do not automatically become properties on `window`, which
reduces accidental global-name conflicts.  Modules are deferred automatically
and can import other modules:

```javascript
// __STATIC__/js/simulation.js
import {stepModel} from "./model.js";

document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
  const output = document.getElementById("nm-output");
  if (output) output.textContent = stepModel();
});
```

Use relative module paths for files stored together.  If modules come from an
external CDN, consider availability, version pinning, privacy, integrity, and
the site's Content Security Policy before making the course depend on them.

<catsoop-section>Libraries CAT-SOOP May Already Load</catsoop-section>

The standard CAT-SOOP template establishes the global `catsoop` object and
loads several built-in packages, including MathJax, syntax-highlighting code,
diagram support, and dark-mode support.  The default question handler also
loads its AJAX code.

Question types can request additional scripts only when they are used.  In
particular, a `pythoncode` question using its ACE interface adds CAT-SOOP's
bundled `BASE/js/ace/ace.js` to the page head.

RequireJS is not necessarily present on every CAT-SOOP page, but a custom
question type, plugin, or course preload may add it.  Within `cs_scripts`,
markup inherited from course preloads normally precedes headers later added by
question types.  A RequireJS tag added in `preload.py` can therefore run before
the ACE tag requested by a `pythoncode` question.

Before adding a library:

1. inspect the rendered page head or browser Network panel;
2. search CAT-SOOP and the active course question types for the library;
3. avoid loading a second copy or a different version;
4. check which globals the library defines; and
5. test a page containing all question types and plugins that will coexist
   with the new script.

Loading a second ACE build, for example, can replace the global `ace` object or
change its module paths after `pythoncode` has configured it.  Editors may
then fail only on pages that contain both the custom code and a `pythoncode`
question.

<catsoop-subsection>ACE and RequireJS</catsoop-subsection>

ACE and RequireJS need particular care because both have historically used
AMD-style global names such as `define` and `require`.  CAT-SOOP's bundled ACE
contains its own packaged module loader and detects a global `requirejs`.
Consequently:

* loading RequireJS before ACE can change or prevent ACE's normal
  initialization;
* loading RequireJS after ACE can replace globals that ACE installed; and
* adding another ACE distribution to compensate can leave two incompatible
  copies on the same page.

A page with both an AMD-based custom question type and a `pythoncode` editor
therefore cannot assume that either load order is harmless.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
Do not add RequireJS or another ACE build globally in the course-root
<code>preload.py</code> without testing every affected question type.  A
conflict may appear only on pages that combine the two libraries, making it
easy to miss during development.
</div>

If both are genuinely required:

* limit the setup to the narrowest page-specific `preload.py`;
* use versions and a no-conflict or namespaced build known to work together;
* write the intended load order explicitly rather than relying on qtype order;
* avoid changing `window.define`, `window.require`, `window.requirejs`, or
  `window.ace` after initialization; and
* test editor creation, submission, rerendering, and the other widget after
  every CAT-SOOP or library upgrade.

There is no universal preload snippet that makes arbitrary ACE and RequireJS
versions compatible.  Prefer an ES module or a dependency with no AMD globals
for new page-level code when that is an option.

<catsoop-section>Avoiding Conflicts in Course Code</catsoop-section>

Place course globals under a unique namespace rather than defining generic
names such as `setup`, `state`, or `data`:

```javascript
window.numericalMethods = window.numericalMethods || {};

window.numericalMethods.setupConvergence = function () {
  // Course-specific setup.
};
```

Do not add properties to CAT-SOOP's `catsoop` object unless an extension API
explicitly calls for it.  Prefix DOM IDs and CSS classes as well—for example,
`nm-convergence-plot` and `nm-expanded`—to avoid collisions with CAT-SOOP or a
question type.

Initialization should also be idempotent when possible:

```javascript
document.querySelectorAll("[data-nm-toggle]").forEach((element) => {
  if (element.dataset.nmInitialized === "yes") return;
  element.dataset.nmInitialized = "yes";
  element.addEventListener("click", () => {
    element.classList.toggle("nm-active");
  });
});
```

This matters on interactive question pages, where parts of a question can be
rerendered after checking or submitting.

<catsoop-section>Debugging</catsoop-section>

When a script does not work:

1. open the browser console and fix the first JavaScript error;
2. check the Network panel for a 404 or blocked script;
3. inspect the rendered `src` URL to confirm `CURRENT` or `COURSE` resolution;
4. verify that the target element exists when initialization runs;
5. look for duplicate script tags and global `define`, `require`, or `ace`
   changes; and
6. reproduce the issue on a minimal page with the same qtypes.

Keep browser code free of secrets.  JavaScript source, embedded data, and
values placed in the DOM are visible to every user who can load the page.
